WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.169
Modern applications often expose security-sensitive behavior through APIs used by web, mobile, and service clients.

2
00:00:08.169 --> 00:00:21.515
REST APIs, GraphQL concepts at a high level, JSON request and response handling, API authentication, authorization, input validation, error handling, and rate limiting all shape runtime risk.

3
00:00:21.515 --> 00:00:26.934
A DAST program that only follows visible links may miss important API behavior.

4
00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:30.574
API documentation and schemas can improve coverage.

5
00:00:30.574 --> 00:00:42.787
OpenAPI descriptions, request examples, collections, or schema-driven testing concepts help scanners understand endpoints, parameters, methods, and expected data shapes.

6
00:00:42.787 --> 00:00:46.670
Documentation still needs validation against reality.

7
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:56.699
An API may expose undocumented paths, require specific authorization context, or behave differently depending on tenant, role, or object state.

8
00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:00.177
Object-level access control is a core API concern.

9
00:01:00.177 --> 00:01:09.560
Testing should consider whether users can access only the records, tenants, objects, files, or resources they are authorized to access.

10
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:17.567
This is difficult for generic scanners because it requires multiple identities and meaningful data relationships.

11
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:23.957
DAST can support the evidence, but application knowledge and test design are often required.

12
00:01:23.957 --> 00:01:29.295
Frontend-heavy applications add browser behavior and client-side routing.

13
00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:39.163
A scanner may need to execute JavaScript, maintain state, follow dynamic navigation, and understand backend requests generated by the client.

14
00:01:39.163 --> 00:01:44.582
Service integrations also matter because APIs often call downstream systems.

15
00:01:44.582 --> 00:01:53.237
DAST must adapt to how the application actually works rather than assuming every target is a simple collection of static pages.
